Name     oe-never;
PartNo   00;
Date     2026-01-01;
Revision 01;
Designer decpld;
Company  decpld;
Assembly none;
Location none;
Device   g22v10;

/* SPEC.md 7.4, output enable: always DISABLED.

   The decisive design. deCPLD encodes a permanently-off pad as an
   output-enable row with every link INTACT -- constantly false -- and
   that encoding has never been checked against the oracle. If WinCUPL
   writes the same thing here, the inference behind
   `supports_input_only` becomes a measurement.

   A `.pld` may not compile at all: a constantly-disabled output is
   arguably a design error, and CUPL may say so. That refusal is itself
   evidence, and `EXPECT refusal` is not asserted here precisely because
   which way this goes is the question. */

PIN 2  = i0;
PIN 23 = o0;

o0    = i0;
o0.oe = 'b'0;
